            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A succession of worldly empires or kingdoms, traditionally identified as Babylon, Medo-Persia, Greece, and Rome, which will ultimately be destroyed and replaced by God's eternal kingdom.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Daniel 2:31-45\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A succession of empires ending with Greece (Babylon, Media, Persia, Greece), a view held primarily by critical scholars who date the book of Daniel to the Maccabean period.\",\"basis\":[\"Daniel 2:31-45\",\"Daniel 7:1-8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A representation of the general qualitative, moral, and political decline of human government over time, paralleling classical myths like Hesiod's Ages of Man.\",\"basis\":[\"Daniel 2:37-43\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A strictly eschatological or futuristic alignment of empires culminating in a revived Roman Empire or end-times global government (represented by the iron and clay feet).\",\"basis\":[\"Daniel 2:41-43\",\"Revelation 13:1-10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While there is near unanimous agreement that the metals symbolize successive human kingdoms because Daniel explicitly states this to Nebuchadnezzar, debate exists over the exact historical identification of the later metals. The traditional Christian view sees Rome as the fourth kingdom, whereas modern critical scholarship often identifies the fourth kingdom as Greece.\"}",
